  "contracts": {
    "lem-tdmath-local-contraction-flow": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$. Suppose every partial derivative $\\partial_jf$ exists on a neighbourhood of $a\\in U$ and is continuous at $a$. Then $f$ is totally differentiable at $a$, and $Df(a)$ is the linear map with matrix $Jf(a)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be convex and open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at every point. If $M\\ge0$ satisfies $\\|Df(z)v\\|_2\\le M\\|v\\|_2$ for every $z\\in U$ and $v\\in\\mathbb R^m$, then\n\n$$\\|f(y)-f(x)\\|_2\\le M\\|y-x\\|_2\\qquad(x,y\\in U).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $f$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By openness choose $R>0$ with $\\overline B(q_0,2R)\\subset U$. Continuity of $X$ and $DX$ at $q_0$ gives finite $M,L\\ge0$ bounding $\\|X\\|$ on $\\overline B(q_0,R)$ and $\\|DX\\|$ on $B(q_0,2R)$. By [F1] $X$ is totally differentiable there, and [F2] applied to the convex ball $B(q_0,2R)$ gives $\\|X(u)-X(v)\\|\\le L\\|u-v\\|$ for $u,v\\in\\overline B(q_0,R)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\mathcal C$ be the closed ball in $C([-\\delta,\\delta],\\mathbb R^m)$ of curves with values in $\\overline B(q_0,R)$, with the supremum norm. It is complete: a Cauchy sequence converges uniformly componentwise because $\\mathbb R^m$ is complete; its uniform limit is continuous and remains in the closed ball. For $p\\in\\overline B(q_0,R/2)$ define $T_p\\gamma(t):=p+\\int_0^t X(\\gamma(s))\\,ds$. The integrand is continuous and hence integrable by [F3]. Choose $\\delta>0$ with $M\\delta\\le R/2$ and $L\\delta<1$. Then $T_p$ maps $\\mathcal C$ into itself, since $\\|T_p\\gamma(t)-q_0\\|\\le\\|p-q_0\\|+M|t|\\le R$, and is a contraction with constant at most $L\\delta$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Starting at the constant curve $\\gamma_0(t)=p$, iterate $\\gamma_{k+1}=T_p\\gamma_k$. If $\\kappa=L\\delta<1$, then $\\|\\gamma_{k+1}-\\gamma_k\\|_\\infty\\le\\kappa^k\\|\\gamma_1-\\gamma_0\\|_\\infty$; the geometric tail bound makes $(\\gamma_k)$ uniformly Cauchy. Its limit $\\phi(\\cdot,p)\\in\\mathcal C$ is a fixed point by the contraction bound. Any two fixed points have distance at most $\\kappa$ times that distance, so they coincide.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "For $g(s)=X(\\phi(s,p))$, continuity and [F3] make every component Riemann integrable. At an interior $t$, the difference quotient of $\\phi(t,p)=p+\\int_0^t g(s)\\,ds$ differs from $g(t)$ by at most $\\sup_{|s-t|\\le|h|}\\|g(s)-g(t)\\|$, which tends to zero by continuity; thus $\\partial_t\\phi=X(\\phi)$. Conversely any $C^1$ solution staying in the ball satisfies the fixed-point equation by [F4], so uniqueness of the fixed point is uniqueness of the IVP.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "claim": "If $p,p'$ lie in $\\overline B(q_0,R/2)$, subtract their fixed-point equations from step 3.1 and take suprema to obtain $\\|\\phi(\\cdot,p)-\\phi(\\cdot,p')\\|_\\infty\\le\\|p-p'\\|+L\\delta\\|\\phi(\\cdot,p)-\\phi(\\cdot,p')\\|_\\infty$. Since $L\\delta<1$, division gives the local Lipschitz estimate $(1-L\\delta)^{-1}\\|p-p'\\|$.",
          "step": "4.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Whenever the compositions remain in the chosen ball, both $t\\mapsto\\phi(t+s,p)$ and $t\\mapsto\\phi(t,\\phi(s,p))$ solve the same IVP at $t=0$, with initial value $\\phi(s,p)$. Uniqueness from step 4.1 gives the local flow law; setting $t=-s$ gives the local inverse relation.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No base point q0 exists in an empty domain."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The zero vector field is covered by its unique constant fixed point, constructed by the contraction iteration in step 3.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement covers m=1; the same Euclidean ball and scalar contraction estimates apply in step 2.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "At t=0 the fixed-point equation gives phi(0,p)=p, verified in step 4.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The ODE derivative is asserted on the open time interval with endpoint behavior supplied by the continuous integral equation in step 4.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The Picard iterates form one recursively defined sequence and converge by a geometric bound in step 3.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lemma is an existence and uniqueness assertion, not an iff statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lemma is an existence and uniqueness assertion, not an iff statement."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-tdmath-variational-flow-and-commutation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-local-contraction-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, $m\\ge1$, let $X:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ have continuous first partial derivatives, and fix $q_0\\in U$. There are $r,\\delta>0$ and a map $\\phi:[-\\delta,\\delta]\\times\\overline B(q_0,r/2)\\to\\overline B(q_0,r)$ such that for each $p\\in\\overline B(q_0,r/2)$, $t\\mapsto\\phi(t,p)$ is $C^1$, satisfies $\\phi(0,p)=p$ and $\\partial_t\\phi(t,p)=X(\\phi(t,p))$ on $(-\\delta,\\delta)$, and is the unique such solution that stays in $\\overline B(q_0,r)$. The dependence on the initial point is locally Lipschitz, and the local flow law $\\phi(t,\\phi(s,p))=\\phi(t+s,p)$ holds whenever the displayed maps are defined in the chosen neighborhood.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $f$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-heine-cantor-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ be a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]]), let\n$(Y,d_Y)$ be any metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to Y$ be\ncontinuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then $f$ is uniformly continuous\n([[def-metric-uniform-continuity]]).\n\nNo choice principle is used: the cover built below is cut out by a property, and\nthe Lebesgue number lemma it is fed to is itself choice free\n([[thm-lebesgue-number-lemma]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $p$ near $q$ put $\\Delta_h(t):=\\phi_X^t(p+h)-\\phi_X^t(p)$. The integral equations from [F1] and the one-variable fundamental theorem applied to $\\theta\\mapsto X(\\phi_X^s(p)+\\theta\\Delta_h(s))$ give\n$$\\Delta_h(t)=h+\\int_0^t A_h(s)\\Delta_h(s)\\,ds,\\quad A_h(s):=\\int_0^1DX(\\phi_X^s(p)+\\theta\\Delta_h(s))\\,d\\theta.$$\nThe segments lie in $K$ after shrinking the initial ball and time interval. The local Lipschitz estimate in [F1] gives $\\sup_t\\|\\Delta_h(t)\\|=O(\\|h\\|)$. Uniform continuity of $DX$ on $K$ from [F4] makes $A_h(s)$ converge uniformly to $DX(\\phi_X^s(p))$ as $h\\to0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The linear integral equation $J(t)=I+\\int_0^tDX(\\phi_X^s(p))J(s)\\,ds$ has a unique continuous solution: on the complete space $C([ -\\delta,\\delta],M_m(\\mathbb R))$, the map $T(J)(t)=I+\\int_0^tDX(\\phi_X^s(p))J(s)\\,ds$ has contraction constant at most $L\\delta$. Its closed ball of radius $(1-L\\delta)^{-1}$ maps into itself because $1+L\\delta(1-L\\delta)^{-1}=(1-L\\delta)^{-1}$; the contraction iteration therefore converges uniformly to its unique fixed point. Define $R_h(t):=\\Delta_h(t)-J(t)h$. Subtracting the integral equations and writing the integrand difference as $(A_h-DX(\\phi_X^s(p)))\\Delta_h+DX(\\phi_X^s(p))R_h$ gives\n$$\\|R_h\\|_\\infty\\le L\\delta\\|R_h\\|_\\infty+\\delta\\|A_h-DX(\\phi_X^s(p))\\|_\\infty\\|\\Delta_h\\|_\\infty=o(\\|h\\|).$$\nThus $D_q\\phi_X^t(p)=J(t,p)$. The continuous integrand in the equation for $J$ has average over $[t,t+h]$ tending to its value at $t$: the quotient error is at most $\\sup_{|s-t|\\le|h|}\\|DX(\\phi_X^s(p))J(s,p)-DX(\\phi_X^t(p))J(t,p)\\|$, which tends to zero. Thus $\\partial_tJ=DX(\\phi_X^t(p))J$ and $J(0,p)=I$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Write $J_h(s)=D_q\\phi_X^s(p+h)$ and $J_0(s)=D_q\\phi_X^s(p)$. Subtracting their integral equations gives\n$$J_h-J_0=\\int_0^t DX(\\phi_h)(J_h-J_0)\\,ds+\\int_0^t(DX(\\phi_h)-DX(\\phi_0))J_0\\,ds,$$\nwhere $\\phi_h=\\phi_X^s(p+h)$ and $\\phi_0=\\phi_X^s(p)$. Boundedness of $D^2X$ on $K$ and $\\phi_h-\\phi_0=O(\\|h\\|)$ bound the second integral by $C\\|h\\|$ for a fixed $C$; the first integral has norm at most $L\\delta\\|J_h-J_0\\|_\\infty$. Since $L\\delta<1$, absorption gives $J_h-J_0=O(\\|h\\|)$ uniformly in time. Uniform continuity of $D^2X$ on $K$ and the integral mean-value formula give, uniformly in $s$,\n$$DX(\\phi_h)-DX(\\phi_0)=D^2X(\\phi_0)[\\phi_h-\\phi_0,\\cdot]+o(\\|h\\|)=D^2X(\\phi_0)[J_0h,\\cdot]+o(\\|h\\|),$$\nusing $\\phi_h-\\phi_0=J_0h+o(\\|h\\|)$ from step 2.1. Also $J_h=J_0+O(\\|h\\|)$. Substitute these expansions in the subtracted equation: the forcing term is\n$$\\int_0^tD^2X(\\phi_0)[J_0h,J_0(\\cdot)]\\,ds+o(\\|h\\|),$$\nuniformly in time. For each $h$, let $K_h$ solve the linear integral equation obtained by retaining this displayed leading term and the coefficient $DX(\\phi_0)$; existence and uniqueness follow from the same contraction bound $L\\delta<1$. Then $R_h:=J_h-J_0-K_h$ satisfies $\\|R_h\\|_\\infty\\le L\\delta\\|R_h\\|_\\infty+o(\\|h\\|)$, so $R_h=o(\\|h\\|)$. Thus $D_pJ[h](v)=K_h(v)$, and, writing $K_2[u,v]=D_q^2\\phi_X[u,v]$, it is the unique solution\n$$K_2(t)[u,v]=\\int_0^t\\big(DX(\\phi_X^s)K_2(s)[u,v]+D^2X(\\phi_X^s)[J(s)u,J(s)v]\\big)\\,ds.$$\nThe integral equation makes $K_2$ bilinear in $(u,v)$; the same contraction estimate and continuity of its coefficients give continuous dependence on $(t,p)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "Let $V(t):=Y(\\phi_X^t(q))$ and $W(t):=D_q\\phi_X^t(q)Y(q)$. By [F2] and the variational equation, $V'=DY(\\phi_X^t)X(\\phi_X^t)$ and $W'=DX(\\phi_X^t)W$. The identity $[X,Y]=0$ makes $V$ solve this same linear equation with $V(0)=W(0)=Y(q)$; uniqueness from the contraction argument gives $D_q\\phi_X^t(q)Y(q)=Y(\\phi_X^t(q))$.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\partial_t\\phi_X=X(\\phi_X)$, the chain rule [F2] gives the continuous mixed derivative $\\partial_tD_q\\phi_X=DX(\\phi_X)J$; differentiating in $t$ gives the continuous derivative $\\partial_t^2\\phi_X=DX(\\phi_X)X(\\phi_X)$. Steps 2.1 and 2.1 give the continuous first and second derivatives in $q$, so every first- and second-order partial derivative in $(t,q)$ exists and is continuous. Hence the local flow is jointly $C^2$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For fixed $t$, the curve $s\\mapsto\\phi_X^t(\\phi_Y^s(q))$ has derivative $D\\phi_X^t\\,Y(\\phi_Y^s(q))=Y(\\phi_X^t(\\phi_Y^s(q))$ by step 3.2, and starts at $\\phi_X^t(q)$. It is therefore the $Y$-flow from that initial point; IVP uniqueness from [F1] yields $\\phi_X^t\\phi_Y^s(q)=\\phi_Y^s\\phi_X^t(q)$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.2",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "There is no base point or local flow on an empty domain."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For the zero field the flow is identity, J=I by step 2.1, and bracket transport is the identity in step 3.2."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
