          "reason": "The result is an implication from a vanishing bracket to commuting flows."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No converse from commuting flows to a vanishing bracket is asserted."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-tdmath-exact-multiple-implies-pfaff-integrability": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-one-form-and-horizontal-path",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ be open, $n\\ge1$, with coordinates $(x_1,\\ldots,x_n,z)$. A $C^2$ one-form on $U$ is a field of linear functionals on tangent vectors of the form $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i(x,z)\\,dx_i+b(x,z)\\,dz$, where every coefficient is twice continuously differentiable. At $q\\in U$, it acts by $\\omega_q(u_1,\\ldots,u_n,w)=\\sum_i a_i(q)u_i+b(q)w$. It is nonsingular on $U$ when $\\omega_q\\ne0$ for every $q$; then $H_q:=\\ker\\omega_q$ is an $n$-dimensional subspace of $T_qU\\cong\\mathbb R^{n+1}$.\n\nFor one-forms $\\alpha,\\beta$, their exterior product is the alternating two-form $(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)(u,v)=\\alpha(u)\\beta(v)-\\alpha(v)\\beta(u)$. For a one-form $\\alpha$ and a two-form $\\sigma$, $(\\alpha\\wedge\\sigma)(u,v,w)=\\alpha(u)\\sigma(v,w)-\\alpha(v)\\sigma(u,w)+\\alpha(w)\\sigma(u,v)$. The exterior derivative of the displayed $C^2$ form is the $C^1$ two-form\n\n$$d\\omega=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}(\\partial_i a_j-\\partial_j a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dx_j+\\sum_{i=1}^n(\\partial_i b-\\partial_z a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dz.$$\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ path is a continuous $\\gamma:[s_0,s_1]\\to U$ with $s_0\\le s_1$ and admitting a finite partition on each closed subinterval of which it is $C^1$. It is $\\omega$-horizontal when $\\omega_{\\gamma(t)}(\\dot\\gamma(t))=0$ on the interior of each such subinterval. Endpoints where a derivative is one-sided are included by continuity. For an open $V\\subseteq U$, $p$ is horizontally reachable from $q$ within $V$ if a horizontal path in $V$ joins $q$ to $p$. Both signs of time and finite concatenations are allowed by this definition.\n\nOn a neighborhood $W$, an integrating factor for $\\omega$ is a nowhere-zero $\\mu\\in C^1(W)$ for which $\\mu\\omega=dS$ for some $S\\in C^2(W)$ with $dS$ nowhere zero. This definition is local; it asserts neither a global potential on $U$ nor a physical identification or calibration of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $f$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-heine-cantor-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ be a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]]), let\n$(Y,d_Y)$ be any metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to Y$ be\ncontinuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then $f$ is uniformly continuous\n([[def-metric-uniform-continuity]]).\n\nNo choice principle is used: the cover built below is cut out by a property, and\nthe Lebesgue number lemma it is fed to is itself choice free\n([[thm-lebesgue-number-lemma]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $q\\in U$ and distinct coordinates $i,j$. Choose $h,k>0$ small enough that the coordinate rectangle from $q$ to $q+he_i+ke_j$ lies in $U$, and set $\\Delta=S(q+he_i+ke_j)-S(q+he_i)-S(q+ke_j)+S(q)$. By [F4] the second partials are uniformly continuous on the chosen closed rectangle. Applying [F2] to coordinate segments and [F3] to their continuous derivatives, Newton–Leibniz gives\n$$\\Delta=\\int_0^h\\!\\int_0^k S_{ij}(q+se_i+te_j)\\,dt\\,ds=\\int_0^k\\!\\int_0^h S_{ji}(q+se_i+te_j)\\,ds\\,dt.$$\nEach equality follows by taking the two endpoint differences in the outer coordinate and then applying Newton–Leibniz to the inner difference; both integrands are continuous by $S\\in C^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Divide both expressions in step 1.1 by $hk$ and let $h,k\\downarrow0$. Continuity of $S_{ij}$ and $S_{ji}$ makes each average tend to its value at $q$, so $S_{ij}(q)=S_{ji}(q)$. Since $q,i,j$ were arbitrary, the coordinate formula in [F1] gives $d(dS)=0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Put $\\lambda:=1/\\mu\\in C^1(U)$. Then $\\omega=\\lambda dS$. Expanding the coordinate derivative by the product rule and using step 2.1 yields $d\\omega=d\\lambda\\wedge dS$. Therefore $\\omega\\wedge d\\omega=\\lambda\\,dS\\wedge d\\lambda\\wedge dS=0$, because the alternating product vanishes when the same one-form $dS$ occurs twice.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "There is no point at which a form or potential is evaluated on an empty domain."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
