        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-tdmath-local-caratheodory-inaccessibility": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-one-form-and-horizontal-path",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ be open, $n\\ge1$, with coordinates $(x_1,\\ldots,x_n,z)$. A $C^2$ one-form on $U$ is a field of linear functionals on tangent vectors of the form $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i(x,z)\\,dx_i+b(x,z)\\,dz$, where every coefficient is twice continuously differentiable. At $q\\in U$, it acts by $\\omega_q(u_1,\\ldots,u_n,w)=\\sum_i a_i(q)u_i+b(q)w$. It is nonsingular on $U$ when $\\omega_q\\ne0$ for every $q$; then $H_q:=\\ker\\omega_q$ is an $n$-dimensional subspace of $T_qU\\cong\\mathbb R^{n+1}$.\n\nFor one-forms $\\alpha,\\beta$, their exterior product is the alternating two-form $(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)(u,v)=\\alpha(u)\\beta(v)-\\alpha(v)\\beta(u)$. For a one-form $\\alpha$ and a two-form $\\sigma$, $(\\alpha\\wedge\\sigma)(u,v,w)=\\alpha(u)\\sigma(v,w)-\\alpha(v)\\sigma(u,w)+\\alpha(w)\\sigma(u,v)$. The exterior derivative of the displayed $C^2$ form is the $C^1$ two-form\n\n$$d\\omega=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}(\\partial_i a_j-\\partial_j a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dx_j+\\sum_{i=1}^n(\\partial_i b-\\partial_z a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dz.$$\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ path is a continuous $\\gamma:[s_0,s_1]\\to U$ with $s_0\\le s_1$ and admitting a finite partition on each closed subinterval of which it is $C^1$. It is $\\omega$-horizontal when $\\omega_{\\gamma(t)}(\\dot\\gamma(t))=0$ on the interior of each such subinterval. Endpoints where a derivative is one-sided are included by continuity. For an open $V\\subseteq U$, $p$ is horizontally reachable from $q$ within $V$ if a horizontal path in $V$ joins $q$ to $p$. Both signs of time and finite concatenations are allowed by this definition.\n\nOn a neighborhood $W$, an integrating factor for $\\omega$ is a nowhere-zero $\\mu\\in C^1(W)$ for which $\\mu\\omega=dS$ for some $S\\in C^2(W)$ with $dS$ nowhere zero. This definition is local; it asserts neither a global potential on $U$ nor a physical identification or calibration of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-local-contraction-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, $m\\ge1$, let $X:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ have continuous first partial derivatives, and fix $q_0\\in U$. There are $r,\\delta>0$ and a map $\\phi:[-\\delta,\\delta]\\times\\overline B(q_0,r/2)\\to\\overline B(q_0,r)$ such that for each $p\\in\\overline B(q_0,r/2)$, $t\\mapsto\\phi(t,p)$ is $C^1$, satisfies $\\phi(0,p)=p$ and $\\partial_t\\phi(t,p)=X(\\phi(t,p))$ on $(-\\delta,\\delta)$, and is the unique such solution that stays in $\\overline B(q_0,r)$. The dependence on the initial point is locally Lipschitz, and the local flow law $\\phi(t,\\phi(s,p))=\\phi(t+s,p)$ holds whenever the displayed maps are defined in the chosen neighborhood.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-variational-flow-and-commutation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a $C^2$ vector field on an open subset of $\\mathbb R^m$, where $C^2$ means that total derivatives through order two exist and are continuous. Its local flow $\\phi_X^t(q)$ from [[lem-tdmath-local-contraction-flow]] is $C^2$ jointly in $(t,q)$, and $J(t,q):=D_q\\phi_X^t(q)$ satisfies $\\partial_tJ=DX(\\phi_X^t(q))J$ and $J(0,q)=I$. For two such fields define their bracket by $[X,Y](q):=DY(q)X(q)-DX(q)Y(q)$. If $[X,Y]=0$ on a neighborhood and all local maps stay there, then $D_q\\phi_X^t(q)Y(q)=Y(\\phi_X^t(q))$ and their local flows commute: $\\phi_X^t\\circ\\phi_Y^s=\\phi_Y^s\\circ\\phi_X^t$ whenever both sides are defined.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-codimension-one-frobenius",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$, $n\\ge1$, be open and let $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i dx_i+b dz$ be a $C^2$ one-form with $\\omega_q\\ne0$ near $q_0\\in U$. After shrinking to a neighborhood on which one coefficient $b$ is nonzero, the following are equivalent: (i) $\\omega\\wedge d\\omega=0$ throughout that neighborhood; (ii) on a possibly smaller neighborhood there are $\\mu\\in C^1$ nowhere zero and $S\\in C^2$ with $dS=\\mu\\omega$. The theorem is local; it gives no global exactness or thermodynamic calibration.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here “local graph dimension $k$” means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-integrable-horizontal-orbits-have-empty-interior",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$, $n\\ge1$, be open, and let $\\omega$ be a nowhere-zero $C^2$ one-form, with the horizontal paths of [[def-tdmath-one-form-and-horizontal-path]]. Assume that every point has a neighborhood $W$ with $S\\in C^2(W)$ and nowhere-zero $\\mu\\in C^1(W)$ satisfying $dS=\\mu\\omega$. For every $q\\in U$, its horizontal reachability class in $U$ is contained in a countable union of compact subsets of $U$ with empty interior. Consequently every nonempty open $V\\subseteq U$ contains a point not horizontally reachable from $q$ by any horizontal path in $U$. In particular, if $q\\in V$, that point is not reachable by a path contained in $V$. No global first integral or choice axiom is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $c_i=-a_i/b$ and $X_i=\\partial_{x_i}+c_i\\partial_z$. These $C^2$ fields form a basis of $\\ker\\omega$. Their bracket has only a vertical component, $[X_i,X_j]^z=\\partial_i c_j+c_i\\partial_zc_j-\\partial_jc_i-c_j\\partial_zc_i$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Direct coordinate expansion gives $d\\omega(X,Y)=X[\\omega(Y)]-Y[\\omega(X)]-\\omega([X,Y])$ for $[X,Y]=DY\\,X-DX\\,Y$. Suppose for contradiction that $[X_i,X_j](q)\\ne0$ at some $q$. For small $t$, let $Y_t=(\\phi_i^t)_*X_j$, so $Y_t(q)=D\\phi_i^t(\\phi_i^{-t}(q))X_j(\\phi_i^{-t}(q))$. The flow and variational equations give $\\phi_i^{-t}(q)=q-tX_i(q)+o(t)$ and $D\\phi_i^t(\\phi_i^{-t}(q))=I+tDX_i(q)+o(t)$; differentiability of $X_j$ gives $X_j(\\phi_i^{-t}(q))=X_j(q)-tDX_j(q)X_i(q)+o(t)$. Multiplying yields $Y_t(q)=X_j(q)-t[X_i,X_j](q)+o(t)$. Since $[X_i,X_j](q)=v\\partial_z$ for some $v\\ne0$ and $b(q)\\ne0$, $\\omega_q([X_i,X_j](q))=vb(q)\\ne0$; hence $\\omega_q(Y_t(q))=-tvb(q)+o(t)\\ne0$ for all sufficiently small nonzero $t$, so $Y_t(q)$ is transverse to $\\ker\\omega_q$. Thus $X_1(q),\\ldots,X_n(q),Y_t(q)$ are linearly independent.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "By continuity, choose an open Euclidean ball $B$ about $q$, contained in the coordinate neighborhood, on which $b$ and the vertical coefficient of the same bracket $[X_i,X_j]$ are both nonzero. For each $r\\in B$, repeat step 2.1 at $r$ with a sufficiently small nonzero $t=t_r$. The flow of $Y_t$ is $\\phi_i^t\\circ\\phi_j^s\\circ\\phi_i^{-t}$, a finite horizontal concatenation with both time signs available. Starting at $r$, compose this flow with those of $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ to obtain a $C^2$ endpoint map $\\Psi_r$ near the zero duration vector. Then $\\Psi_r(0)=r$ and its derivative has the independent columns $X_1(r),\\ldots,X_n(r),Y_t(r)$. Choose $|t|$ and the duration neighborhood small enough that every intermediate segment stays inside $B$, using continuity of local flows and that $r$ is interior to $B$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "At each $r\\in B$, apply [L1] to $H_r(s,p)=\\Psi_r(s)-p$ at $(0,r)$, with ambient dimension $2(n+1)$ and target dimension $n+1$, permuting the input to $(p,s)$ so $s$ is solved. Its solved derivative is invertible by step 3.1. Conditionally, some open neighborhood $O_r\\subseteq B$ of $r$ consists of endpoints $\\Psi_r(s(p))$, all reachable from $r$ by horizontal paths inside $B$. Reachability inside $B$ is an equivalence relation: constant paths give reflexivity, path reversal gives symmetry, and finite concatenation gives transitivity. Every equivalence class is open in $B$, since each of its points $r$ has $O_r$ in that same class. Two distinct classes would separate the connected Euclidean ball $B$ into one nonempty open class and its nonempty open complement, a union of other classes. Thus $B$ is one class: every point of $B$ is reachable from $q$ by a path inside $B$, contradicting (a) with $V=B$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Thus every bracket $[X_i,X_j]$ vanishes by the contradiction at step 4.1. The coordinate identity from step 2.1 gives $d\\omega(X_i,X_j)=-\\omega([X_i,X_j])=0$, hence $(\\omega\\wedge d\\omega)(\\partial_z,X_i,X_j)=0$ for every pair. These evaluations determine the three-form: modulo the $n$-plane $\\ker\\omega$, the tangent space has dimension one, so every triple reduces by multilinearity and alternation to a multiple of one $\\partial_z$ and two horizontal vectors. Therefore $\\omega\\wedge d\\omega=0$. By [F4], condition (b) follows, conditionally on [L1].",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra",
            "L1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "Conversely suppose (b) holds at every point of $U$. The exact global reachability lemma [F8] applies to these local first integrals and the declared horizontal paths. For every $q\\in U$ and every open neighborhood $V\\subseteq U$ of $q$, it gives a point $p\\in V$ not horizontally reachable from $q$ even by a path in $U$, and therefore not by a path in $V$. This proves (a), including neighborhoods larger than an individual first-integral chart, and completes the equivalence.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F8"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem concerns points q in an open state domain."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The one-form is assumed nowhere zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For n=1 every pairwise bracket vanishes by alternation in step 2.1; the endpoint and local-potential construction still apply in steps 3.1–5.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and proof: Steps3.1–4.1 use a genuinely nonzero bracket on one ball for the contradiction. In step6.1 the exact orbit lemma excludes a point from the entire reachability class, including zero-duration paths and paths leaving any particular first-integral chart."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and proof: Steps3.1–4.1 keep every intermediate flow segment inside the connected ball B and exhaust B by the unique open orbit. Step6.1 uses the global orbit lemma for every prescribed open V, even when V exceeds one local chart."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and proof: Finite fields/coordinate minors and deterministic local flows give the forward argument. The converse invokes the choice-free finite-word/rational-box/nested-ball lemma; no Countable Choice or atlas selection is introduced."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Local inaccessibility rules out nonzero brackets and then gives the Pfaff condition conditionally through steps 1.1–5.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement and proof: Step6.1 applies the exact proper-orbit lemma to first integrals at every point, obtaining an unreachable point in every open V, without requiring paths to stay in one first-integral chart."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-tdmath-two-dimensional-pfaff-factor": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-one-form-and-horizontal-path",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ be open, $n\\ge1$, with coordinates $(x_1,\\ldots,x_n,z)$. A $C^2$ one-form on $U$ is a field of linear functionals on tangent vectors of the form $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i(x,z)\\,dx_i+b(x,z)\\,dz$, where every coefficient is twice continuously differentiable. At $q\\in U$, it acts by $\\omega_q(u_1,\\ldots,u_n,w)=\\sum_i a_i(q)u_i+b(q)w$. It is nonsingular on $U$ when $\\omega_q\\ne0$ for every $q$; then $H_q:=\\ker\\omega_q$ is an $n$-dimensional subspace of $T_qU\\cong\\mathbb R^{n+1}$.\n\nFor one-forms $\\alpha,\\beta$, their exterior product is the alternating two-form $(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)(u,v)=\\alpha(u)\\beta(v)-\\alpha(v)\\beta(u)$. For a one-form $\\alpha$ and a two-form $\\sigma$, $(\\alpha\\wedge\\sigma)(u,v,w)=\\alpha(u)\\sigma(v,w)-\\alpha(v)\\sigma(u,w)+\\alpha(w)\\sigma(u,v)$. The exterior derivative of the displayed $C^2$ form is the $C^1$ two-form\n\n$$d\\omega=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}(\\partial_i a_j-\\partial_j a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dx_j+\\sum_{i=1}^n(\\partial_i b-\\partial_z a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dz.$$\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ path is a continuous $\\gamma:[s_0,s_1]\\to U$ with $s_0\\le s_1$ and admitting a finite partition on each closed subinterval of which it is $C^1$. It is $\\omega$-horizontal when $\\omega_{\\gamma(t)}(\\dot\\gamma(t))=0$ on the interior of each such subinterval. Endpoints where a derivative is one-sided are included by continuity. For an open $V\\subseteq U$, $p$ is horizontally reachable from $q$ within $V$ if a horizontal path in $V$ joins $q$ to $p$. Both signs of time and finite concatenations are allowed by this definition.\n\nOn a neighborhood $W$, an integrating factor for $\\omega$ is a nowhere-zero $\\mu\\in C^1(W)$ for which $\\mu\\omega=dS$ for some $S\\in C^2(W)$ with $dS$ nowhere zero. This definition is local; it asserts neither a global potential on $U$ nor a physical identification or calibration of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "$dS=e^xy\\,dx+e^x\\,dy=e^x(dy+y\\,dx)=\\mu\\omega$, and $S_y=e^x>0$, so $dS$ and $\\mu$ are nowhere zero.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For any piecewise-$C^1$ horizontal path $\\gamma$, the identity in step 1.1 and [F1]–[F2] give $(S\\circ\\gamma)'=dS(\\dot\\gamma)=\\mu\\omega(\\dot\\gamma)=0$ on each smooth piece; therefore $S$ is constant along every finite concatenation of horizontal pieces.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
