    "cex-tdmath-closed-punctured-plane-form-is-not-exact": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-one-form-and-horizontal-path",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ be open, $n\\ge1$, with coordinates $(x_1,\\ldots,x_n,z)$. A $C^2$ one-form on $U$ is a field of linear functionals on tangent vectors of the form $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i(x,z)\\,dx_i+b(x,z)\\,dz$, where every coefficient is twice continuously differentiable. At $q\\in U$, it acts by $\\omega_q(u_1,\\ldots,u_n,w)=\\sum_i a_i(q)u_i+b(q)w$. It is nonsingular on $U$ when $\\omega_q\\ne0$ for every $q$; then $H_q:=\\ker\\omega_q$ is an $n$-dimensional subspace of $T_qU\\cong\\mathbb R^{n+1}$.\n\nFor one-forms $\\alpha,\\beta$, their exterior product is the alternating two-form $(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)(u,v)=\\alpha(u)\\beta(v)-\\alpha(v)\\beta(u)$. For a one-form $\\alpha$ and a two-form $\\sigma$, $(\\alpha\\wedge\\sigma)(u,v,w)=\\alpha(u)\\sigma(v,w)-\\alpha(v)\\sigma(u,w)+\\alpha(w)\\sigma(u,v)$. The exterior derivative of the displayed $C^2$ form is the $C^1$ two-form\n\n$$d\\omega=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}(\\partial_i a_j-\\partial_j a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dx_j+\\sum_{i=1}^n(\\partial_i b-\\partial_z a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dz.$$\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ path is a continuous $\\gamma:[s_0,s_1]\\to U$ with $s_0\\le s_1$ and admitting a finite partition on each closed subinterval of which it is $C^1$. It is $\\omega$-horizontal when $\\omega_{\\gamma(t)}(\\dot\\gamma(t))=0$ on the interior of each such subinterval. Endpoints where a derivative is one-sided are included by continuity. For an open $V\\subseteq U$, $p$ is horizontally reachable from $q$ within $V$ if a horizontal path in $V$ joins $q$ to $p$. Both signs of time and finite concatenations are allowed by this definition.\n\nOn a neighborhood $W$, an integrating factor for $\\omega$ is a nowhere-zero $\\mu\\in C^1(W)$ for which $\\mu\\omega=dS$ for some $S\\in C^2(W)$ with $dS$ nowhere zero. This definition is local; it asserts neither a global potential on $U$ nor a physical identification or calibration of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-scalar-and-vector-line-integrals-along-piecewise-c1-paths",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be piecewise-$C^1$. If $a=b$, define both line integrals below to be $0$. If $a<b$, choose an admissible partition $a=t_0<\\cdots<t_m=b$ and a continuous derivative extension $v_i$ on each piece. Let $f$ be a continuous scalar field and $F$ a continuous vector field on a set containing the trace of $\\gamma$. The **scalar line integral with respect to arc length** and the **vector-field line integral** are\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma f\\,ds:=\\sum_{i<m}\\int_{t_i}^{t_{i+1}}f(\\gamma(t))\\lVert v_i(t)\\rVert_2\\,dt,$$\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma F\\cdot d\\mathbf r:=\\sum_{i<m}\\int_{t_i}^{t_{i+1}}\\langle F(\\gamma(t)),v_i(t)\\rangle\\,dt,$$\n\nwhere the inner product is [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. The summands exist by [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]. Independence of the admissible partition is proved in [[lem-line-integrals-are-independent-of-the-piecewise-c1-partition]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-gradient-theorem-for-line-integrals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $\\phi:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be $C^1$, and let $\\gamma:[a,b]\\to U$ be piecewise-$C^1$. Then\n\n$$\\int_\\gamma\\nabla\\phi\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\phi(\\gamma(b))-\\phi(\\gamma(a)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n([[def-continuity-real]]). Then $f$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $D=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}$ and $\\omega=P\\,dx+Q\\,dy$ with $P(x,y)=-y/(x^2+y^2)$ and $Q(x,y)=x/(x^2+y^2)$. The domain $D$ is open since each nonzero point has a ball avoiding the origin, and the coefficients are smooth with $(P,Q)\\ne(0,0)$ at every point. It is path-connected: if the segment joining $p,q\\in D$ avoids $0$, use that segment; if it contains $0$, then $p,q,0$ are collinear, and a nonzero vector $r$ perpendicular to $p$ makes both segments $[p,r]$ and $[r,q]$ avoid $0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Writing $r^2=x^2+y^2$, direct differentiation gives $\\partial_xQ=(y^2-x^2)/r^4=\\partial_yP$, so $d\\omega=(\\partial_xQ-\\partial_yP)dx\\wedge dy=0$ on $D$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The unit circle $\\gamma(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, lies in the domain and uses the form defined in step 1.1. Along it $(P,Q)=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\gamma'(t)$, hence [F2] gives $\\int_\\gamma\\omega=\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$ by [F4].",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The closedness is proved in step 2.1. If $\\omega=dS$ globally on $D$, then $F=\\nabla S$ for the corresponding vector field, and [F3] would give $\\int_\\gamma\\omega=S(\\gamma(2\\pi))-S(\\gamma(0))=0$, contradicting step 2.2. Thus $\\omega$ is closed but not globally exact. This does not assert that every possible global integrating factor is absent.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "discharge-contradiction"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The punctured plane is nonempty."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The coefficient vector (-y,x)/(x^2+y^2) never vanishes on the punctured plane, step 1.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The witness is two-dimensional."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The loop is closed with coinciding endpoints, and its period remains 2pi by step 2.2."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The gradient theorem applies to the closed smooth loop with equal endpoints in step 3.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The form and unit-circle loop are explicitly specified in steps 1.1 and 2.2."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The counterexample refutes a universal closed-implies-exact claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The counterexample refutes a universal closed-implies-exact claim."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-tdmath-integrable-horizontal-orbits-have-empty-interior": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-one-form-and-horizontal-path",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ be open, $n\\ge1$, with coordinates $(x_1,\\ldots,x_n,z)$. A $C^2$ one-form on $U$ is a field of linear functionals on tangent vectors of the form $\\omega=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i(x,z)\\,dx_i+b(x,z)\\,dz$, where every coefficient is twice continuously differentiable. At $q\\in U$, it acts by $\\omega_q(u_1,\\ldots,u_n,w)=\\sum_i a_i(q)u_i+b(q)w$. It is nonsingular on $U$ when $\\omega_q\\ne0$ for every $q$; then $H_q:=\\ker\\omega_q$ is an $n$-dimensional subspace of $T_qU\\cong\\mathbb R^{n+1}$.\n\nFor one-forms $\\alpha,\\beta$, their exterior product is the alternating two-form $(\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)(u,v)=\\alpha(u)\\beta(v)-\\alpha(v)\\beta(u)$. For a one-form $\\alpha$ and a two-form $\\sigma$, $(\\alpha\\wedge\\sigma)(u,v,w)=\\alpha(u)\\sigma(v,w)-\\alpha(v)\\sigma(u,w)+\\alpha(w)\\sigma(u,v)$. The exterior derivative of the displayed $C^2$ form is the $C^1$ two-form\n\n$$d\\omega=\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}(\\partial_i a_j-\\partial_j a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dx_j+\\sum_{i=1}^n(\\partial_i b-\\partial_z a_i)\\,dx_i\\wedge dz.$$\n\nA piecewise-$C^1$ path is a continuous $\\gamma:[s_0,s_1]\\to U$ with $s_0\\le s_1$ and admitting a finite partition on each closed subinterval of which it is $C^1$. It is $\\omega$-horizontal when $\\omega_{\\gamma(t)}(\\dot\\gamma(t))=0$ on the interior of each such subinterval. Endpoints where a derivative is one-sided are included by continuity. For an open $V\\subseteq U$, $p$ is horizontally reachable from $q$ within $V$ if a horizontal path in $V$ joins $q$ to $p$. Both signs of time and finite concatenations are allowed by this definition.\n\nOn a neighborhood $W$, an integrating factor for $\\omega$ is a nowhere-zero $\\mu\\in C^1(W)$ for which $\\mu\\omega=dS$ for some $S\\in C^2(W)$ with $dS$ nowhere zero. This definition is local; it asserts neither a global potential on $U$ nor a physical identification or calibration of $S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-local-contraction-flow",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, $m\\ge1$, let $X:U\\to\\mathbb R^m$ have continuous first partial derivatives, and fix $q_0\\in U$. There are $r,\\delta>0$ and a map $\\phi:[-\\delta,\\delta]\\times\\overline B(q_0,r/2)\\to\\overline B(q_0,r)$ such that for each $p\\in\\overline B(q_0,r/2)$, $t\\mapsto\\phi(t,p)$ is $C^1$, satisfies $\\phi(0,p)=p$ and $\\partial_t\\phi(t,p)=X(\\phi(t,p))$ on $(-\\delta,\\delta)$, and is the unique such solution that stays in $\\overline B(q_0,r)$. The dependence on the initial point is locally Lipschitz, and the local flow law $\\phi(t,\\phi(s,p))=\\phi(t+s,p)$ holds whenever the displayed maps are defined in the chosen neighborhood.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-tdmath-variational-flow-and-commutation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a $C^2$ vector field on an open subset of $\\mathbb R^m$, where $C^2$ means that total derivatives through order two exist and are continuous. Its local flow $\\phi_X^t(q)$ from [[lem-tdmath-local-contraction-flow]] is $C^2$ jointly in $(t,q)$, and $J(t,q):=D_q\\phi_X^t(q)$ satisfies $\\partial_tJ=DX(\\phi_X^t(q))J$ and $J(0,q)=I$. For two such fields define their bracket by $[X,Y](q):=DY(q)X(q)-DX(q)Y(q)$. If $[X,Y]=0$ on a neighborhood and all local maps stay there, then $D_q\\phi_X^t(q)Y(q)=Y(\\phi_X^t(q))$ and their local flows commute: $\\phi_X^t\\circ\\phi_Y^s=\\phi_Y^s\\circ\\phi_X^t$ whenever both sides are defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with\n\n$$f(b) - f(a) \\;=\\; f'(c)\\,(b - a) .$$\n\nEquivalently, since $b - a \\ne 0$, there is $c \\in (a,b)$ at which\n$f'(c) = \\bigl(f(b)-f(a)\\bigr)/(b-a)$: the derivative somewhere inside equals\nthe average rate of change across the whole interval.\n\n**Continuity on the closed interval cannot be dropped.** Differentiability at\nevery point of $(a,b)$ alone does not suffice: a function on $[0,1]$,\ndifferentiable at every point of $(0,1)$ with derivative constantly $1$, for\nwhich no $c$ works, is exhibited later on this page as a false statement, and\nthe companion page works the same witness out in full.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-local-implicit-rank",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $1\\le q\\le n$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, and let $F:U\\to\\mathbb R^q$ be $C^1$ in the sense of [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Suppose $F(x_*)=0$ and some $q\\times q$ minor of the derivative matrix $DF(x_*)$ is invertible. This nonsingular-minor hypothesis is the explicit full-row-rank condition used here. If $q<n$, then after permuting coordinates $x=(u,v)\\in\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, there are open neighborhoods $P$ of $u_*$ and $Q$ of $v_*$ and a unique $C^1$ map $\\varphi:P\\to Q$ such that\n\n$$F(u,v)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad v=\\varphi(u)\\qquad((u,v)\\in P\\times Q).$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$D\\varphi(u)=-D_vF(u,\\varphi(u))^{-1}D_uF(u,\\varphi(u)).$$\n\nThus the local zero set is a graph over $n-q$ free coordinates. If $q=n$, the zero is locally isolated, so the local graph dimension is zero. Here “local graph dimension $k$” means a local graph parameterized by an open subset of $\\mathbb R^k$, with a singleton counted as dimension zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-connected-subsets-of-r-are-intervals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $E \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. Then $E$ is connected ([[def-connected-r]]) if and\nonly if $E$ is **order-convex** ([[def-interval]]), that is, if and only if\n\n$$x, z \\in E \\text{ and } x \\le w \\le z \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; w \\in E .$$\n\n**On the word \"interval\".** Order-convexity is exactly the defining property\nthat [[def-interval]] proves for each of its nine forms, and in that sense the\ntheorem says that the connected subsets of $\\mathbb{R}$ are the intervals. The\nconverse classification, that every order-convex subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ is empty\nor one of the nine forms, is true and is explicitly **not proved anywhere in\nthis library**; [[def-interval]] records that omission in its own remarks. So\nthe statement proved below is the equivalence with order-convexity, and the\nphrase \"is an interval\" is to be read as \"is order-convex\" throughout this page.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-rationals-countable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\mathbb{Q} \\approx \\mathbb{N}$ ([[def-equinumerous]]): the rationals are\ncountably infinite ([[def-countable]]).\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The one place where a reader expects a choice,\n\"pick a representative $a/b$ of each rational\", is exactly where\n[[lem-rat-positive-denominator]] applies: every rational *has* a representative\nwith positive denominator, so the map $(a,b) \\mapsto [(a,b)]$ defined on\n$\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ is already **surjective** onto $\\mathbb{Q}$,\nand countability follows from a surjection without ever selecting a\nrepresentative. The same device handles $\\mathbb{Z}$, which is a surjective image\nof $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ by construction ([[def-integers]]).",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Write $\\mathbb{Q}_{\\mathbb{R}}$ for the image of $\\mathbb{Q}$ in $\\mathbb{R}$\nunder the canonical embedding $q \\mapsto \\hat q$ ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), the\nset usually written $\\mathbb{Q}$ once the identification is made, and put\n$X := \\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\mathbb{Q}_{\\mathbb{R}}$ for the irrationals. Then:\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Q}_{\\mathbb{R}}$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}$, that is,\n   $\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}_{\\mathbb{R}}} = \\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]);\n2. $X$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}$;\n3. every nonempty open subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable\n   ([[def-countable]]).\n\nClaim 2 is not a symmetry of claim 1: the rationals are dense because they are\n*constructed* to approximate, whereas the irrationals are dense because there are\ntoo many points in any interval for a countable set to exhaust it, which is why\nclaim 3 is proved alongside and used for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces ([[def-metric-space]]) and let\n$f : X \\to Y$ be continuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then:\n\n1. If $(X,d_X)$ is compact ([[def-metric-compactness]]), the image\n   $f[X] \\subseteq Y$ is a compact subset of $Y$.\n2. More generally, if $K \\subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$, then $f[K]$ is\n   a compact subset of $Y$.\n\nNo choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $K \\subseteq X$ be a\ncompact subset ([[def-metric-compactness]]). Then $K$ is closed in $X$\n([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n\nNo choice principle is used: both covers below are given by a rule, and the\nindexed form of [[lem-compactness-is-intrinsic]] returns indices rather than\nsets.\n\n**The converse is false in general.** A closed and bounded subset of an arbitrary\nmetric space need not be compact\n([[fs-closed-and-bounded-implies-compact-in-every-metric-space]]); it is exactly\nin $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that the converse holds ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-space-complete",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$ with the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]) is a complete metric space\n   ([[def-complete-metric-space]]).\n2. Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on\n   $\\mathbb{R}^n$ ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ is complete.\n\n**The hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited and is not decoration.**\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] defines $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its three metrics only for\n$n \\ge 1$, because at $n = 0$ the metric $d_\\infty$ would be a maximum over the\nempty index set. Every statement about $\\mathbb{R}^n$ in this library carries the\nhypothesis, and this one does too.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
