          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Use the local first integrals in the Statement. Write $\\omega=\\sum_{j=1}^{n+1}\\omega_j dx_j$ in the fixed Euclidean coordinates and set $w=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_{n+1})$ and $Z_j=e_j-(\\omega_j/|w|^2)w$. These globally defined $C^2$ vector fields are horizontal and span $\\ker\\omega$ at each point, since their matrix is the orthogonal projection onto that kernel. On every neighborhood supplied by the hypothesis, $dS=\\mu\\omega$ has nonzero derivative, and each $Z_j$-flow preserves $S$ as long as its trajectory remains there, by [F3]. Its derivative therefore preserves horizontal tangent vectors there: differentiating $S(\\phi_j^t(x))=S(x)$ gives $dS_{\\phi_j^t(x)}D\\phi_j^t(x)v=dS_xv=0$ for horizontal $v$. The compact image of any finite flow trajectory has a finite subcover by such neighborhoods; a sufficiently fine finite subdivision of its compact time interval puts each subtrajectory in one of them. This follows directly from compactness: otherwise shrinking subintervals failing the subdivision would have a limiting time whose neighborhood contains them. Composing the local derivative identities obtains preservation over the full duration. Local flow uniqueness makes these continuations consistent, and [F2] gives $C^2$ dependence on durations and initial points wherever the finite composition is defined.",
