          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Fix $q\\in U$, and let $\\mathcal O(q)$ consist of endpoints of all finite words of the $n+1$ fields $Z_j$, with signed durations and intermediate trajectories in $U$; include the empty word. At any $r$, choose $n$ of these fields forming a basis of $\\ker\\omega_r$. In a neighborhood from the hypothesis, [L1] applied to $S(x)-S(r)$ gives a local graph plaque with $n$ free coordinates. Composing the $n$ chosen flows for small durations gives endpoints in that plaque. The derivative at zero, followed by projection to the plaque's free coordinates, is invertible, because those vectors form its tangent basis; apply [L1] to this free-coordinate endpoint map minus a target, with durations solved, to see that the endpoints contain a plaque neighborhood of $r$. All these paths stay in the chosen neighborhood. A horizontal piecewise $C^1$ path preserves the local $S$ by [F3], so near every one of its parameter values it lies in this plaque neighborhood and hence in the same finite-word orbit as that point. The finite-word orbits form equivalence classes by reversal and concatenation, and their preimages along the path are consequently relatively open in its interval domain. By [F4], connectedness of that interval forces one class. Thus every point horizontally reachable from $q$ lies in $\\mathcal O(q)$; the reverse inclusion follows because each word is a horizontal concatenation.",
